"""
TIMIT -> the SRT tree and paths list the evaluation reads.

    python evaluation/dataset/timit_parser.py          # from the repository root

One `.srt.json` per clip under `tmp/data/timit_data/`, and `tmp/paths/timit_paths_all.json`
listing them. A clip is one sentence, so every SRT holds one segment:

    {"labels": {"phoneme_set": "std/latin", "lang": "en", "lang_group": "latin",
                "n_tokens": 208, "gold_fingerprint": "..."},
     "text": "...",
     "segments": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 2.49, "text": "...",
                   "boundaries": [150, 205, ...],
                   "words": [{"w": "chocolate", "s": 0.1506, "e": 0.4727, ...,
                              "phones": ["ch", "aa", "kcl", "k", ...],
                              "local_ph": [9, 34, 26, 11, 11, ...], "ph_count": [2, 1, 1, 1, ...],
                              "gold_phg": [4, 7, 3, 4, 4, ...],
                              "boundaries": [205, 299, 313, 362, ...]}]}]}

`boundaries` (ms from the segment start) is what the scorer reads: where every phone that
lies inside a word starts, from TIMIT's hand-labelled `.PHN` file.

The phone LABELS are written in the std phoneme set - latin local ids, the label space of the
`mbfa-latin` models - by `std_labels.py`, which says how each of TIMIT's 61 symbols is written
and why (evaluation/LABEL_MAPPING.md walks through it). In a word, `phones` is what the transcriber wrote, `local_ph` the same phones as
token ids, and `ph_count` how many tokens each phone became (2 for a diphthong or an
affricate), so `boundaries[i]` is the end of the `ph_count[i]` tokens that phone `i` owns.
"""

